-- title:
--   Rigidity for bialgebras with étale Cartier dual
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative local ring with maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$. Let $H$ be a commutative ring carrying an $R$-bialgebra structure whose comultiplication is cocommutative, and which is finite and free as an $R$-module; assume moreover that [`CartierDual R H`](def/HopfAlgebra_CartierDual.html#L12), by definition the $R$-linear dual $\operatorname{Hom}_R(H,R)$ with its induced commutative ring structure, is étale as an $R$-algebra. Let $H'$ be a second commutative ring with a cocommutative $R$-bialgebra structure, again finite and free over $R$. Let $\varphi \colon H' \to H$ be a homomorphism of $R$-bialgebras, and suppose that for every $a \in H'$ the element $\varphi(a) - \varepsilon(a)\cdot 1_H$, where $\varepsilon$ is the counit of $H'$ and $\varepsilon(a)\cdot 1_H$ denotes the image of $\varepsilon(a)$ under the structure map $R \to H$, lies in the ideal of $H$ generated by the image of $\mathfrak m$, i.e. in $\mathfrak m H$. The conclusion is that $\varphi(a) = \varepsilon(a)\cdot 1_H$ holds exactly, for every $a \in H'$; that is, $\varphi$ is the composite of the counit of $H'$ with the structure map $R \to H$.
--
--   In the language of schemes this is the rigidity statement for finite locally free commutative group schemes of multiplicative type over a local ring: a homomorphism $\operatorname{Spec} H \to \operatorname{Spec} H'$ whose reduction to the residue field is trivial is itself trivial, the multiplicative-type hypothesis being expressed by étaleness of the Cartier dual. It is used in the comparison of Néron models of modular Jacobians at $p$, where maps out of a multiplicative-type subgroup scheme are pinned down by their special fibre.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_HopfAlgebra_bialgHom_apply_eq_algebraMap_counit_of_etale_cartierDual_of_sub_mem_map_maximalIdeal.lean

